<!-- candlekeep:start v=__VERSION__ -->
<IMPORTANT>
You have CandleKeep — the user's curated knowledge library, an authoritative source for topics they keep books on (design, UI/UX, security, architecture, agent development, code review, etc.). For a task that involves real judgment in one of those domains, spawn `candlekeep-librarian` first (subagent at `~/.codex/agents/candlekeep-librarian.toml`) — it is cheap (gpt-5.6-luna, low effort, read-only) and returns silently if nothing relevant exists. File reads and repo exploration can run in parallel afterwards.

## Spawn the librarian first for:
- code review / "review my X" / "find improvements" / "audit Y"
- UI/UX critique / design feedback / accessibility review
- security audit / threat modeling
- architecture decisions / "best practices for" / "how should I structure"
- agent development / prompt engineering / LLM workflows
- "research X" / "what does the literature say" / "compare A vs B"

## Skip the library — proceed directly — for:
- Trivial or mechanical edits: typo fix, syntax error, simple rename, lint, formatting
- Work outside any book domain: git operations, terminal commands, file moves, build/config plumbing
If a task is small or clearly unrelated to the domains above, don't spawn the librarian — just do the work.

## After the librarian returns:
Spawn `candlekeep-item-reader` (in a single `multi_tool_use.parallel` batch) for each relevant book. Cite findings inline as (Book Title, p. N). End your answer with the CandleKeep citation block format defined in `~/.agents/skills/candlekeep/SKILL.md`.

## Release agents when done — they are one-shot:
After you consume a librarian's or item-reader's output, release it with Codex's agent-close primitive (`close_agent`). Codex counts completed-but-open sub-agents against its concurrency limit until released; leaking them is what makes a later `spawn_agent` fail with `agent thread limit reached`. A turn that spawned a librarian and N readers must end with all of them released.

## If `spawn_agent` fails with `agent thread limit reached`:
That is a **local Codex sub-agent cap, NOT a CandleKeep quota** — never call it a "CandleKeep limit" and never suggest upgrading. Recover: release any completed CandleKeep agents, retry the spawn ONCE; if it still fails, read inline yourself with read-only `ck items list/toc/read --no-session` (see SKILL.md "Subagent failure handling"), and stop attempting spawns for the rest of the session — go straight to inline. Keep the library-grounded answer.

Skill body: `~/.agents/skills/candlekeep/SKILL.md`
Subagents:  `~/.codex/agents/candlekeep-{librarian,item-reader,book-writer,book-enricher}.toml`
</IMPORTANT>
<!-- candlekeep:end -->
